You can use the `focus()` method on the element returned by `getElementsByTagName`. Here is an example:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
elements[0].focus(); // focus on the first element
```
This will focus on the first `<input>` element found in the document.

If you want to focus on a specific element, you can loop through the elements and check the `id` or `className` attributes to find the one you want:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
for (var i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
  if (elements[i].id === "myInput") {
    elements[i].focus();
    break;
  }
}
```
Alternatively, you can use `querySelector` or `querySelectorAll` which are more powerful and flexible than `getElementsByTagName`:
```
var myInput = document.querySelector("#myInput"); // focus on the element with id="myInput"
myInput.focus();

var inputs = document.querySelectorAll("input[name='myInput']"); // focus on the element with name="myInput"
inputs[0].focus();
```
Note that `querySelector` and `querySelectorAll` return a NodeList, which is an array-like object, so you can use indexing and `forEach` method to work with it.
